
The paper develops one connected identification-and-design theory. Finite reference calibration is treated as part of the uncertainty budget rather than as fixed metadata. Unrestricted missing outcomes are preserved as a sharp identified set. The information margin of the proxy becomes both an inverse condition number and a design parameter. Multisite pooling is analyzed at the estimand level, leading to a necessary-and-sufficient condition for point identification of the population-average latent prevalence and a sharp set when that condition fails. Finite-sample resolution is then translated into an explicit sample-size law. Finally, we separate two release problems that are often conflated: a same-data width gate, which controls marginal erroneous releases but does not automatically preserve nominal conditional coverage, and an independent pilot/confirmatory design that does preserve confirmatory coverage among released designs.

This distinction is practically important. A quantity can be identifiable yet too imprecise for the intended scientific statement. A pooled observable can be perfectly measured yet identify the wrong weighted combination of site-specific latent states. A data-dependent abstention rule can improve practical resolution while changing the conditioning event on which coverage is assessed. Partial identification treats these outcomes as information rather than failure \cite{manski2005,obradovic2024}, while selective prediction recognizes abstention as a legitimate action when evidence is insufficient \cite{geifman2017,angelopoulos2023}. The framework developed here connects those ideas to a measurement-engineering workflow in which uncertainty is exposed rather than hidden.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Measurement model and finite calibration uncertainty}

Let \(Z \in \{ 0,1\}\) be a declared latent target and \(Y \in \{ 0,1\}\) an observable proxy. Define latent prevalence \(\pi = P(Z = 1)\), sensitivity \(\alpha = P(Y = 1 \mid Z = 1)\), specificity \(\beta = P(Y = 0 \mid Z = 0)\), and proxy-positive rate \(q = P(Y = 1)\). Then

\[q = \alpha\pi + (1 - \beta)(1 - \pi) = (1 - \beta) + J\pi,\]

with Youden information margin

\[J = \alpha + \beta - 1.\]

When \(J > 0\) and calibration is known in the deployment domain,

\[\pi = \frac{q + \beta - 1}{J}.\]

The algebraic inverse is elementary, but the scientific problem is not. In practice, \(q\), \(\alpha\), and \(\beta\) are estimated from finite samples. Let \(\widehat{q}\), \(\widehat{\alpha}\), and \(\widehat{\beta}\) be independent binomial proportions from deployment, target-positive calibration, and target-negative calibration samples. Allocating a total failure probability \(\delta\) equally across the three Hoeffding events gives radius

\[\epsilon(n,\delta/3) = \sqrt{\frac{\log(6/\delta)}{2n}}.\]

The simultaneous intervals define a rectangular uncertainty set \(Q \times A \times B\). The latent identified set is

\[\Pi(Q,A,B) = \left\{ \frac{q + \beta - 1}{\alpha + \beta - 1}:q \in Q,\alpha \in A,\beta \in B,\alpha + \beta - 1 > 0 \right\} \cap [ 0,1].\]

If the simultaneous box contains the true triple, then the true \(\pi\) belongs to this set. The finite-sample guarantee therefore comes from the joint calibration event, not from plugging point estimates of sensitivity and specificity into the inverse.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{fig1_calibration.pdf}
\caption{Finite calibration uncertainty. The mean width of the outer latent-prevalence interval contracts with the number of reference calibration observations per class. Values are generated by the deterministic V6 experiment at Research III snapshot 7a2a1a3a.}
\label{fig:calibration}
\end{figure}

The canonical stress experiment shows how strongly finite reference data can control usable resolution. Mean interval width is 0.960 with 50 calibration observations per class, 0.622 at 250, 0.479 at 500, 0.372 at 1,000, and 0.281 at 2,500 (Figure~\ref{fig:calibration}). Every canonical run is identifiable and conditional coverage among identified runs is 1.00 in this fixed-seed grid. That empirical value is not a universal coverage theorem. The point is engineering: a pipeline that reports a corrected prevalence to two decimal places while treating estimated calibration parameters as constants can claim more resolution than its reference data support.

\subsection{Missing outcomes and the exact information-loss law}

Suppose \(N\) proxy measurements were intended, \(n_{o}\) are observed, \(s_{o}\) observed values are positive, and \(m = N - n_{o}\) are missing. Without a missingness assumption, the full-sample number of positive outcomes can range from \(s_{o}\) to \(s_{o} + m\), giving the sharp observable-rate set

\[q \in \left[ \frac{s_{o}}{N},\frac{s_{o} + m}{N} \right].\]

The endpoints are attainable by assigning every missing outcome zero or one. No uniformly tighter bound is justified without additional information about the missing values. If calibration is known and \(J > 0\), the inverse is monotone in \(q\), so the proxy interval maps directly to a sharp latent interval after compatibility clipping.

The V11 result makes the information loss explicit. If both mapped endpoints remain in the interior of \([ 0,1]\), the exact latent identified-set width is

\[\boxed{W_{miss} = \frac{m/N}{J}}.\]
